6. Maintain architecture and performance

Follow existing project conventions.

The combat engine determines what abilities do. The UI only describes them.

Keep this separation intact.

* js/data/characters.js: character-specific icon metadata.
* js/ui/mobile-abilities.js: icon and preview resolution.
* js/game/touch-controls.js: display and touch handling.
* Battle and Practice screens: current player-state synchronization.
* js/game/combat-ai.js: CPU decision and targeting behavior.
* js/game/character.js: fighter state, facing, combat physics.
* js/core/touch-layout.js: available controls and saved layout validation.
* styles.css: presentation.

Optimize icon updates by caching the last displayed grounded/airborne state and preview.

Preserve the existing sprite-image reuse behavior wherever possible.

Do not replace entire button trees unnecessarily, because doing so could interfere with held inputs or multi-touch pointer ownership.

Do not introduce new render loops, unnecessary DOM allocations, or redundant image loading.

